 \section{Conclusion}
 
-We study whether benchmark gains from self-evolving persistent skills correspond to retained behavioral improvement and, independently, which lifecycle mechanisms produce those gains. Our audits show that revised skill artifacts can produce real behavioral changes, but selected gains can disappear under fresh deployment and the transfer established so far remains narrow. Feedback, optimizer variation, validation selection, artifact content, execution variability, and verifier choice can each shape the observed endpoint.
+We diagnose where the gains from self-evolving persistent skills reside and how far they extend. All three methods in the standard-task evaluations have positive paired gains in both runs, but task variation separates their utility profiles. SkillEvolver remains positive across representation, instance, and requirement changes; SPARK combines gains on two probes with a decline on instance changes; CoEvoSkills declines on all three. The inspected traces identify decision criteria and correctness checks expressed during successful reuse. Complementary lifecycle interventions show that adopted artifact components can change behavior, selected outcomes can disappear under fresh deployment, and fixed outputs can receive different verdicts under different verifiers. Together, these findings distinguish useful artifacts, their behavioral scope, and the processes that produce or measure their apparent improvement.
 
 A higher benchmark score should therefore be reported only at the strongest level supported by matched evidence: a selected gain, a retained artifact effect, bounded controlled transfer, evolution-pathway dependence, or measurement dependence. We provide a lifecycle audit that links each claim to the required intervention and reporting evidence. Claims of transferable skill learning require frozen parent-to-final improvement that survives fresh deployment and predeclared task variation under valid measurement; weaker evidence should be reported more narrowly.
 
